> Nope: it looks good :)  How does one volunteer for compiling alpha packages?

Assuming you're already a debian maintainer: simply choose a package,
download the stuff, build and upload it before anyone else does :-)

Seriously though, I find that those who built the previous version will
rebuild when a new version comes out, so I generally leave the
out-of-date stuff to those who did it last time, unless it's been a long
time.  Packages which are not yet available at all on Alpha are up for
grabs.

Now with the freeze and beta going on, just make sure that when you're
building a package, the distribution is filled in correctly. Many source
packages have "unstable" in the changelog, and if you don't change that
first, your upload ends up in slink and not in hamm. It's a pain in the
ass to get the correct version in hamm after that.  Also make sure the
version you're uploading for hamm is not of a higher version than the
corresponding i386 hamm version.

> > And who really needs stuff like xemacs and netcdf anyway? (Just kidding :-)
> 
> xemacs is done but requires patches: check out
> 
> <URL: ftp://ftp.ens-lyon.fr/pub/users/LIP/krisrose/Debian/Alpha/hamm/packages/>

I looked at xemacs20_20.4-5.1.diff.gz, but most of what I see is stuff
that is _always_ changed when you build a package. You should diff the
soyrce trees after a make clean or whatever; e.g. "configure" is 3627
lines of diff, but it doesn't make much sense to include that as it'll
get rebuilt anyway. At least, I'm hoping you didn't edit configure by
hand?!

Ah, wait, it is of course the normal source-upload diff.  What I do when
patches are needed, is to move the patched tree to something like
xemacs20-20.4-5.1, and again use dpkg-source to unpack the sources.
Now run "diff -ru xemacs20-20.4-5 xemacs20-20.4-5.1 > xemacs.nmu.diff",
edit out stuff such as "Only in xemacs20-20.4-5.1: *.o" (and configure
diffs!), and then send the resulting diff file to the Bug Tracking
System with subject like "xemacs20: diffs needed for NMU upload for Alpha"
accompanied by a couple of lines why the patches were needed. Perhaps
also suggest that the patches are suitable for general use, or that they
are alpha-specific in which case "#ifdef alpha" around them may be
useful.  I find that in most cases, the "real" maintainer will implement
the diffs without any struggle; most seem happy that their package now
also works on Alpha!

Anyway, upload the package as a binary-only upload (i.e. build with
dpkg-buildpackage -B).  Don't forget the "Distribution"! I see your
tetex-bin_0.9-7.1_alpha.changes has only "unstable" listed! I can't find
your xemacs20.*changes, BTW...

